## Why This Is Asked
Tests React Native bridging fundamentals and lifecycle handling for an intermediate RN developer.

## Key Concepts
- React Native bridge architecture
- Native module lifecycle management
- EventEmitter pattern for cross-platform communication
- Platform-specific implementations (iOS/Android)

## Code Example
```javascript
import { NativeModules, NativeEventEmitter } from 'react-native';
const { BatteryModule } = NativeModules;
const emitter = new NativeEventEmitter(BatteryModule);

// Subscribe to battery updates
const subscription = emitter.addListener('BatteryChange', (event) => {
  console.log('Battery level:', event.level);
  console.log('Is charging:', event.isCharging);
});

// Remove listener when done
subscription.remove();
```

## Implementation Notes

**iOS (Swift):**
- Subclass RCTEventEmitter and override supportedEvents()
- Enable UIDevice.current.isBatteryMonitoringEnabled = true
- Add observers for UIDevice.batteryStateDidChangeNotification and UIDevice.batteryLevelDidChangeNotification
- Call sendEvent(withName:body:) to push updates to JavaScript

**Android (Kotlin):**
- Extend ReactContextBaseJavaModule
- Register BroadcastReceiver in onResume() and unregister in onPause()
- Listen for Intent.ACTION_BATTERY_CHANGED to get current state
- Use device.getIntExtra() to extract level and charging status

**Listener Management:**
- Always clean up event listeners in component unmount to prevent memory leaks
- For class components, store subscriptions as instance variables and remove in componentWillUnmount
- For functional components, return removal function from useEffect
- Consider using the @react-native-community/hooks useDeviceOrientation hook pattern for automatic cleanup

## Why This Is Asked
Gauges candidate ability to architect a small, realistic feature with performance thoughts and React hooks.

## Key Concepts
- In-memory indexing for fast, offline search
- Debounce input and memoize results
- Component memoization to avoid re-renders

## Code Example
```javascript
const EMOJIS = [
  {char:'😀', name:'grinning'},
  {char:'🎉', name:'party'},
  {char:'🚀', name:'rocket'}
];

function useEmojiSearch(query){
  const [q, setQ] = React.useState(query);
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = React.useState(query);
  React.useEffect(() => {
    const t = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(q), 150);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);
  }, [q]);
  const results = React.useMemo(
    () => EMOJIS.filter(e => e.name.toLowerCase().includes(debounced.toLowerCase())),
    [debounced]
  );
  return { query: q, setQuery: setQ, results };
}
```

```javascript
function EmojiPicker(){
  const { query, setQuery, results } = useEmojiSearch('');
  return (
    <>
      <TextInput value={query} onChangeText={setQuery} placeholder="Search emoji" />
      <FlatList
        data={results}
        horizontal
        keyExtractor={e => e.name}
        renderItem={({ item }) => (
          <Text accessibilityLabel={item.name}>{item.char}</Text>
        )}
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle a much larger emoji set or theme switching?
- How would you test the debounce timing and memoization behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests native module design, threading, and lifecycle handling in React Native. It probes how well a candidate bridges real-time events and manages performance and battery impact.

## Key Concepts
- React Native bridge and EventEmitter
- Platform-native timers and dedicated threads
- Lifecycle cleanup and unmount handling
- Backpressure and battery considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
import {NativeModules, NativeEventEmitter} from 'react-native';
const {LiveBadge} = NativeModules;
const em = new NativeEventEmitter(LiveBadge);
let sub = em.addListener('tick', t => {
  console.log('tick', t);
});
LiveBadge.startTicker(1000);
// later
LiveBadge.stopTicker();
sub && sub.remove();
```

